WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.069
A load balancer can improve reliability, but it can also hide problems until users feel them.

2
00:00:06.069 --> 00:00:17.430
Health checks should reflect real readiness: the process is alive, the API responds, the needed model is available, and recent requests are not failing at an unhealthy rate.

3
00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:22.876
A server with an open port but constant generation timeouts is not a healthy target.

4
00:00:22.876 --> 00:00:26.222
Monitor both the front endpoint and every backend.

5
00:00:26.222 --> 00:00:34.003
At the gateway, track request volume, response times, status codes, timeouts, streaming interruptions, and queues.

6
00:00:34.003 --> 00:00:42.173
On each backend, track resource pressure, active requests, model-loaded status, runtime errors, restarts, and failures.

7
00:00:42.173 --> 00:00:47.542
Without backend visibility, you may know the service is slow without knowing why.

8
00:00:47.542 --> 00:00:51.121
Many troubleshooting problems come from mismatches.

9
00:00:51.121 --> 00:01:01.081
A backend may run a different runtime version, expose a different endpoint, use a different quantization, or map the same model name to a different file.

10
00:01:01.081 --> 00:01:03.415
A proxy may strip a required header.

11
00:01:03.415 --> 00:01:06.761
A timeout between layers may interrupt streaming.

12
00:01:06.761 --> 00:01:11.196
Load balancing increases the need to keep assumptions consistent.

13
00:01:11.196 --> 00:01:14.387
Failover and retries should match the workload.

14
00:01:14.387 --> 00:01:17.421
Retrying before a response starts may be safe.

15
00:01:17.421 --> 00:01:21.156
Retrying after streaming begins may confuse the client.

16
00:01:21.156 --> 00:01:26.058
Retrying an agent request that performed tool actions can duplicate work.

17
00:01:26.058 --> 00:01:37.963
Simple dashboards showing backend health, model availability, active requests, failures, and queue growth are enough to tell whether load balancing is helping or hiding trouble.
